## Force Databricks-secret Neo4j password in the deployed app

Context: In a deployed Databricks App the Settings UI let an admin pick
`basic` or `databricks_secret` auth for Neo4j, but `basic` also silently
required a secret behind the scenes (the UI could not accept a typed
password there either), which was confusing and made it look like two
auth paths existed when only one was actually usable. The user asked to
always collect the Neo4j username directly, drop the password field
entirely, and force the password to come from a Databricks secret picked
via two live dropdowns (scope, then key) instead of free-typed strings.

Changes:

1. `src/back/core/databricks/constants.py`
   Add `SECRETS_SCOPES_LIST_PATH`, `SECRETS_LIST_PATH`, `SECRETS_GET_PATH`
   REST API paths for the Databricks Secrets API.
2. `src/back/core/databricks/SecretsService.py` (new)
   `list_scopes`, `list_keys`, `get_secret_value` wrapping the Secrets API,
   mapping 403/404 responses to `InfrastructureError` with remediation text
   (grant `READ` ACL / check the scope-key name).
3. `src/back/core/databricks/DatabricksClient.py`
   Wire up `self.secrets = SecretsService(...)` and add
   `list_secret_scopes` / `list_secret_keys` / `get_secret` facade methods.
4. `src/back/core/graphdb/neo4j/Neo4jConnection.py`
   `DEFAULT_AUTH_METHOD` is now `"databricks_secret"`. `_resolve_auth`
   always requires `username` regardless of method, and for
   `databricks_secret` fetches the password via a new
   `_fetch_databricks_secret` (uses `DatabricksAuth` + `SecretsService`),
   cached per `(scope, key)` for 300s behind a `threading.Lock`
   (`_secret_value_cache`) to avoid hammering the Secrets API on every
   connection. `basic` auth (env var `NEO4J_PASSWORD` or local dev config
   password) still works for backward compatibility but is no longer
   exposed in the UI.
5. `src/back/objects/domain/SettingsService.py`
   `_neo4j_credentials_source` reports whether the password came from a
   Databricks secret, `NEO4J_PASSWORD`, or local config.
   `set_graph_engine_config_result` strips `password` from persisted
   config whenever `auth_method` is `databricks_secret` or `NEO4J_PASSWORD`
   is set, so no clear-text password is ever saved once a live source
   exists. Add `graph_engine_neo4j_secret_scopes_result` /
   `graph_engine_neo4j_secret_keys_result` to back the new dropdowns.
6. `src/api/routers/internal/settings.py`
   Add admin-gated `GET /graph-engine/neo4j-secret-scopes` and
   `GET /graph-engine/neo4j-secret-keys/{scope}`.
7. `src/front/templates/settings.html`
   Drop the `Auth method` selector and the password input. Username is
   now always visible/required. Add `neo4jSecretScope` /
   `neo4jSecretKey` `<select>` dropdowns (each with a refresh button) and
   help text on granting scope `READ` permissions, linking to the updated
   docs.
8. `src/front/static/config/js/settings.js`
   Remove `applyNeo4jAuthMethodVisibility`. Add `loadNeo4jSecretScopes` /
   `loadNeo4jSecretKeys` (populate the two dropdowns live from the new
   endpoints, cascading scope → key). `mergeNeo4jPanelIntoConfigTextarea`
   now always writes `auth_method: "databricks_secret"`, stores
   `secret_scope` / `secret_key`, and deletes `password` outright.
9. `src/front/routes/home.py`
   Remove the now-unused `neo4j_password_from_secret` template variable.
10. `documentation/pr47-neo4j-demo/secret-configuration.md`,
   `documentation/deployment.md`
   Document the secret-only flow: store the password as a Databricks
   secret, grant the app's service principal `READ` on the scope, then
   pick scope/key in Settings. `NEO4J_PASSWORD` / UI `basic` auth are
   noted as deprecated for new configs.
11. `tests/units/graphdb/test_neo4j_store.py`
   `TestDatabricksSecretAuth`: fetches and caches the password via the
   Secrets API, and raises `ValidationError` when `username`,
   `secret_scope`, or `secret_key` is missing.
12. `tests/units/settings/test_neo4j_settings_persistence.py`
   Updated for the new UI contract: hydration loads secret scopes and
   never touches a password field; merge always forces
   `auth_method=databricks_secret` and drops `password`; scope/key
   dropdowns are populated live from the two new endpoints.

